{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/568011-balanced-combat-encounters-for-a-pack-in-2nd-ed","id":568011,"title":"Balanced combat encounters for a pack in 2nd Ed","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Werewolf: The Forsaken"],"posts":[{"date":"2015-06-25T08:38:46-07:00","id":568011,"title":"Balanced combat encounters for a pack in 2nd Ed","content":"Seeing that the mechanics have changed for healing and the war form and the possibility of going into Basu-Ilm which can be pretty powerful. What would be considered a good encounter to challenge a pack (assume 5 werewolves, one if each Auspice). Maybe under the following circumstances<br /> <br /> Easy- Should be no problem for the pack, just a warm up<br /> <br /> Normal- Could be a challenge, the pack is really going to sweat and get scars<br /> <br /> Challenging- One of the pack members might die<br /> <br /> Really challenging - the whole pack might die<br /> <br /> What would be a suitable antagonist(s) in each scenario based on playing experience. In addition, showing where the 5 enemies (werewolves, spirits, hosts, humans, and claimed) are used would be used as an example would be great.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1107-maryshelly","name":"maryshelly","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1107_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"337"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-25T08:45:58-07:00","id":568040,"title":"","content":"It's going to depend a lot on the capability of the werewolves. My werewolf character isn't good at combat, though gauru brings him up to average, but with Command Artifice he's ordered a car full of guys to speed into a guy and the wall behind him.<br /> <br /> Though as a standard, Easy encounters would be a bunch of rank 1 spirits or humans that aren't hunters or otherwise trained for combat. Or one to three rank 2 spirits or hunters.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":886,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","user":"nofather","date":". 06-25-2015, 11:56 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","name":"nofather","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar886_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11016"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-25T10:18:07-07:00","id":568119,"title":"","content":"Yeah, the game doesn't have a difficulty class per encounter. I just go with my gut.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/576-ephsy","name":"Ephsy","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar576_2.png","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2615"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-25T14:44:02-07:00","id":568398,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ephsy</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/568011-balanced-combat-encounters-for-a-pack-in-2nd-ed?p=568119#post568119\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Yeah, the game doesn't have a difficulty class per encounter. I just go with my gut.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> Yeah. It's impossible to just balance per Primal Urge, Renown, Auspice, et cetera. Depends completely on how they are built.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/585-seidmadr","name":"Seidmadr","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar585_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"822"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-25T17:25:15-07:00","id":568518,"title":"","content":"I find NWoD games especially don't really do &quot;Combat balance&quot; any better than the real world does.<br /> <br /> Generally, whoever has the luxury of being the one to start the fight (and thus pick the time and place) has a nearly overwhelming advantage. It is hard to balance things around that.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2007-wade-l","name":"Wade L","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2007_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"136"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-27T08:45:36-07:00","id":570268,"title":"","content":"What about tips for running combat? In the past few encounters my Players and their Pack of characters have done great at finding Bans, Banes etc. and preparing for a Spirit hunt. It was awesome and everything a careful Pack should have been doing.<br /> <br /> However when it came to combat with this Rank 3 Spirit, without using the Bans, Banes etc. and even facing down the Spirit with claws and fangs, they tore it a new one with all of the Werewolf abilities. Tilts got dropped on it from Urshul, making it prone, and got replied by each Werewolf, Gauru healed any damage the Spirit dealt, and grapple contested roles from each in Gauru just shut it out.<br /> <br /> I used Willpower, Essence, Numina, everything I could think of to boost this Spirit into being able to posse any kind of a threat to this four Uratha pack, but mechanically could not. It ended up having twelve dice for Power and was still just getting waylaid and beaten out by the action economy of four to one.<br /> <br /> I realize having more Spirits of lesser rank or allies would help with this, but for when you want the one Big Bad Evil Guy show down, how do you make your antagonists and Spirits last against the onslaught of abilities Uratha now have in 2ed?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4134-gilbod","name":"GilboD","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4134_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"113"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-27T08:55:40-07:00","id":570271,"title":"","content":"In a five on one fight, I'm not surprised it got slaughtered. If you want a one big bad vs. the whole party kind of fight to be challenging, you need to make sure it's <i>really</i> big and <i>really</i> bad. A solo monster in Werewolf should be like a Rank 4 or 5 magath, a bale hound built with way more experience than the party members, a Claimed that's had enough time to transfer most of its Attribute dots over and gained a bunch of Dread Powers, an enormous Rat King, an elder vampire, or even an Idigam.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Going by Willow now, or Wil for short. She/Her/Hers.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","name":"Charlaquin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar269_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10831"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-27T09:06:54-07:00","id":570284,"title":"","content":"Even in 1ed my experience was the only way to challenge werewolves with spirits was thru superior numbers, even weak spirits did some damage then and the combat lasted at least long enough to kill one a turn. Solitary spirits, even rank 4, usually gave one wolf a strong beating if it went first but didn't last long after they retaliated.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6084-2pttakrill","name":"2ptTakrill","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"942"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-27T09:13:52-07:00","id":570290,"title":"","content":"It's worth noting though, that 1e heavily favored strength of numbers - enough toddlers could take down a lone monster, even an extremely powerful one. In 2e, superior numbers is still a big advantage, but ultimately the system usually favors the strongest combatant.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Going by Willow now, or Wil for short. She/Her/Hers.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","name":"Charlaquin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar269_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10831"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-27T10:37:09-07:00","id":570333,"title":"","content":"Yeah an entire pack of werewolves in gauru is going to be a nasty proposition. Ideally they can't hold it for long, I think my guy caps at like 3 or 4 turns before risking Kuruth. But if he decides to go Kuruth there's ten minutes more.<br /> <br /> It's probably okay to have the werewolves stomp some people easily, you don't want a ravenous 10 foot tall monster in something called 'death rage' to hit with pillows strapped to their hands. But you also don't want, 'I turn gauru,' to always be the win response to any threat. So you can either play with numbers, as many spirits will probably have lesser spirits around them, possibly even a higher boss or equal rank partners. Even a single spirit is likely to have hangers-on, feeding off excess Essence. Or you can play canny. Spirits do know about werewolves, running away or otherwise hiding when one turns gauru might be the sensible response, and spirits do have a lot of ways to do that, hiding in zones of influence or changing form and flying away. Worse comes to worse, they can just jump to another world. A single spirit in meatspace who knows they're going to be up against werewolves might level the playing field other ways, limiting the room available for werewolves to maneuver or taking the fight to someplace public.<br /> <br /> Either way, since without a bane you just do bashing damage to spirits, and once it is 'destroyed' it really just goes dormant and hides somewhere until it refreshes enough to come back.<br /> <br /> I was hoping the Hurt Locker would have more mechanical advice about this. As stated before, balance is impossible. One of my first antagonists had a dicepool about the size of the healthiest PC's health, and in one roll managed to take it all out. So I decided a rule was, 'If a dicepool can wipe out all health boxes, it might do so.' But of course eight isn't even that big of a dicepool, comparatively, and it never panned out that way again and for a while antagonists and thugs would just chip away one health box at a time.<br /> <br /> At a certain point, to make reasonable challenges, you'll just need to be aware of the abilities of the player characters, be willing to beef up baddies with passive bonuses and take advantage of the current implications of battle, like if a werewolf goes gauru in the middle of a school an antagonist could lure them into attacking others. As well as the repercussions. Players should exult in gauru but when or if they hit kuruth they should wake up worrying that at some point they may have done something they really wish they didn't. Not that you should punish the player for it but add some gravitas to the choice.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","name":"nofather","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar886_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11016"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-27T10:47:10-07:00","id":570338,"title":"","content":"If you want your characters to suffer, don't have the spirit play fair. Harass with reaching and blast outside of the influence of loci (preferably when they're in the shower). Abduct blooded when they're asleep, Mainly? Don't show the monster.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/576-ephsy","name":"Ephsy","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar576_2.png","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2615"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-07-02T00:41:51-07:00","id":576162,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ephsy</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/568011-balanced-combat-encounters-for-a-pack-in-2nd-ed?p=570338#post570338\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">If you want your characters to suffer, don't have the spirit play fair. Harass with reaching and blast outside of the influence of loci (preferably when they're in the shower). Abduct blooded when they're asleep, Mainly? Don't show the monster.</div> </div> </div> </div>yeah, this guy is correct. What did you expect fighting 4 Ur against 1 spirit of 3rd rank? even 1vs1 it would be an uncertain outcome. Don't make the fight a head on encounter, a single 3rd rank spirit can kill 4 werewolves if played well. night attacks, framed crimes, possessed cultists, house catching a fire, claimed with silver weapons, etc.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4340-warpwind","name":"Warpwind","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4340_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"608"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-07-02T08:20:04-07:00","id":576382,"title":"","content":"Here is my guess, based on mathematics, though mathematics might go out the window given the War form and Kuruth. Let us assume that, in most cases, most werewolves will avoid going into Kuruth, if only because when they finish they will collapse into their human form and be easy prey for anyone who wants to get them, or they might start killing their packmates by accident. <br /> <br /> For werewolves, this would probably be the easiest since you are comparing apples with apples, so an approximate ratio of one to one would be about right. However, if you have a larger pack, that pack would have the advantage since one guy could come out of the war form and allow his buddy to take over while everyone in the other pack is coming out of their rage. If you still assume that the Pure outnumber the Forsaken then there is a possibility that the Pure pack will have one to two more packmembers on average. If they start ganging up on any forsaken then that forsaken might die pretty quickly, war form or not. In addition, if the Pure are not afraid to bite and cause aggravated damage than the battle might be done very quickly.<br /> <br /> For Spirits, I reckon that a rank 2 spirit is a fight for a single werewolf, 2 are a challenge, but can be overcome with the warform and gifts, while 3 rank 2 spirits with Blast will eventually be able to incinerate a Forsaken in Basu-Ilm. Again, this is mostly based on math and statistics. <br /> <br /> For Hosts, this might get complex since they probably favour ambushes and pack tactics. In terms of pure combat, an Azlu would probably be a challenge to a pack if he creates a lair full of webs and attacks them one by one, assuming he has super strenght as well as natural weapons and armour. As for the Rat hosts, one should be no problem, a pack of 5 might give a forsaken in war form a challenge if they swarm him or her.<br /> <br /> As for humans, I assume that a group of humans will probably be easy. Having a half dozen with pistols will probably cause a great deal of damage even to one in the war form. Arming them with silver makes things much more lethal. The write up on Detroit wrote about a group of humans that killed 2 Predator Kings so I imagine that a group of humans with guns and baseball bats can kill werewolves if they act fast and team up on one target at a time.<br /> <br /> For claimed, assuming that they have natural weapons and great strength, and armour, than I assume that one can be a challenge for a werewolf, 2 would be a great challenge and require the war form, and 3 might kill one even if they are in the war form. The claimed dogs in the main book seem pretty tough and capable of swarming a werewolf to cause enough aggrivated damage to bring it down.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1107-maryshelly","name":"maryshelly","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1107_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"337"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-07-02T08:51:35-07:00","id":576435,"title":"","content":"Swarming is great because every wrap-around dot of Aggravated damage a werewolf suffers lowers the amount of lethal damage they need to take to convert another level to Aggravated.<br /> <br /> That is to say:<br /> <br /> If a werewolf has 11 Health Levels, you need to do 12 Lethal in a signle turn to get them to 1 Aggravated. But after that, you need to do 11. And as you progress, you need to do less and less per turn damage to do any lasting damage.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3855-gebbethine","name":"gebbethine","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3855_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"233"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-07-02T09:13:02-07:00","id":576454,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>gebbethine</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/568011-balanced-combat-encounters-for-a-pack-in-2nd-ed?p=576435#post576435\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Swarming is great because every wrap-around dot of Aggravated damage a werewolf suffers lowers the amount of lethal damage they need to take to convert another level to Aggravated.<br /> <br /> That is to say:<br /> <br /> If a werewolf has 11 Health Levels, you need to do 12 Lethal in a signle turn to get them to 1 Aggravated. But after that, you need to do 11. And as you progress, you need to do less and less per turn damage to do any lasting damage.</div> </div> </div> </div>Exactly. Once you get that aggravated damage going then it becomes a downward spiral for the victim. The trick is getting enough damage done to achieve that because it requires tremendous amounts of damage to hurt the war form.<br /> <br /> Another alternative is to find ways to fight around the war form. One problem I had with Werewolf games, and this goes all the way back to Apocalypse, is that the tempting solution to all problems was to turn into the wolf man form and pummel whatever it was that was causing you difficulties. Get a pack pummelling the same target and there was not much out there that could survive. It was a case of having only a hammer in your toolshed and everything starts to look like a nail.<br /> <br /> With the Forsaken rules, one way to circumvent this is to attack in waves. Have one wave of flunkes whose only purpose is to expend the rage of the werewolves. Once they are out of rage, then you can pop them with another group of adversaries that they must fight. Alternatively, put them in a city covered in CCTV cameras and let them know that exposing their forms to humanity will not be tolerated, then throw something at them.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1107-maryshelly","name":"maryshelly","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1107_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"337"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-07-02T09:44:07-07:00","id":576525,"title":"","content":"That seems more loaded down in Apocalypse thought. If you're losing a fight, odds are you're not going to be able to run away, in which case Kuruth is a way good thing, providing you're not taking aggravated every round, not only does it encourage the rest of your pack to do it, but you're working on full heals and your strongest form for so many turns anything else is likely to die.<br /> <br /> I'd also question the math and statistics that say how much of a challenge a rank 2 or 2 rank 2 spirits is going to be. And definitely against humans, since even with silver bullets most people are going to be dealt with in a single Down &amp; Dirty roll. Even if they aren't they're working off of a reduced Defense. With the gauru acting as a bullet dodger, they can probably take down a standard group of six shooters without being brought to serious harm. That's not counting Lunacy or gifts.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":886,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","user":"nofather","date":". 07-02-2015, 12:49 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","name":"nofather","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar886_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11016"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-07-03T15:01:11-07:00","id":577802,"title":"","content":"I don't think a good idea to make the numbers that way, because even in those terms there are a lot of possibilities. In a game I had already an Uratha without no combat training slaugthering six armed goons, without gifts or Lunacy (they had luck and passed the check, the bastards). But they where Mister Average Guys, with 2 on every single relevant trait. And that was 1st edition.<br /> <br /> But them you have to remember that most people aren't average. And the guys that seek problems tend to be problems themselfs. Make the sheets of the spirits or humans in a certain way and you can have the perfect killing machine against your players, or just another piece of junk (battle-wise, I mean). A lone sniper with the right tools can take an entire pack without so much problem, and not even a single supernatural power (just Killing Blows).<br /> <br /> As a rule of thumb for challenge, I assume that the combat isn't the challenge. Werewolfs are born killers, when the battle starts, they are winners already. But context can change this, and that is my challenge to my players. The spirit will not use it's powers to win a battle. It will use them to control context. Even powerful spirits will resort on hit &amp; run, because they don't want to get hurt, even if victory is achieved (they also have their own enemies to worry about, after all).<br /> <br /> Hit &amp; Run is allways a good way to make battles more challenging, if you want to really play the final boss encounter. Well played, even lesser spirits can harm the pack. And remember that with Discorporation death is just another Run. Also heavily emphasise on using tricks to separate the pack. Heading into a bunch of enemies is a bad proposition to anyone, even to werewolfs. The challenge can be the pack remaining united to begin with, or else they will fall one by one.<br /> <br /> Waves and opportunistic enemies are other thing. If your players are specially successful in dealing with their enemies, make those enemies always around waiting for an opportunity. You can increase greatly the threat of a battle just by letting them know that they need to keep enough resources to deal with other enemies, or those enemies are bound to leap on their throats very soon.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-07-03T17:17:44-07:00","id":577986,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>monteparnas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/568011-balanced-combat-encounters-for-a-pack-in-2nd-ed?p=577802#post577802\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I don't think a good idea to make the numbers that way, because even in those terms there are a lot of possibilities. In a game I had already an Uratha without no combat training slaugthering six armed goons, without gifts or Lunacy (they had luck and passed the check, the bastards). But they where Mister Average Guys, with 2 on every single relevant trait. And that was 1st edition.</div> </div> </div> </div>Odd, even in Dalu they'd suffer Lunacy as if they had WP of 8. Mister Average Guys shouldn't be given the Fiat roll to resist Lunacy in 1E.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/576-ephsy","name":"Ephsy","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar576_2.png","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2615"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-07-03T19:43:53-07:00","id":578065,"title":"","content":"I did it because it wasn't average for the character in question (it was a very hard battle), and was a mans to put to the test the sheer strength of an Uratha. Either way, that's hardly the point.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-19T20:01:41-07:00","id":632977,"title":"","content":"Spirits can also boost their stats by rank+2 which means an already tough rank 3 spirit can add on another 10 attack dice and another 5 points to health and defence. Surely that would be a challenge to a werewolf, maybe even a whole pack.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1107-maryshelly","name":"maryshelly","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1107_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"337"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-20T06:23:43-07:00","id":633376,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>maryshelly</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/568011-balanced-combat-encounters-for-a-pack-in-2nd-ed?p=632977#post632977\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Spirits can also boost their stats by rank+2 which means an already tough rank 3 spirit can add on another 10 attack dice and another 5 points to health and defence. Surely that would be a challenge to a werewolf, maybe even a whole pack.</div> </div> </div> </div>Assuming the Spirit has enough Essence to actually throw it around like that (what you just did was spend 20 essence after all). In practice, it doe not happen like that very often.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i><span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman\"><span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman\">The problem with defending the purity of the English language is that English is about as pure as a cribhouse whore. We don't just borrow words; on occasion, English has pursued other languages down alleyways to beat them unconscious and rifle their pockets for new vocabulary. - James. D. Nicoll</span></span></i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/540-tymeaus-jalynsfein","name":"Tymeaus Jalynsfein","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"869"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-20T13:50:10-07:00","id":633761,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>GilboD</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/568011-balanced-combat-encounters-for-a-pack-in-2nd-ed?p=570268#post570268\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I realize having more Spirits of lesser rank or allies would help with this, but for when you want the one Big Bad Evil Guy show down, how do you make your antagonists and Spirits last against the onslaught of abilities Uratha now have in 2ed?</div> </div> </div> </div>Have you tried throwing something from the old Predators book? I have yet to bust em out yet in 2nd edition but in the back there were some epic level nasty antagonists that you could use. After playing with them a bit, they could also be used to give you ideas of what to make yourself to throw at a group. We had some great fun with some of those.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1883-face_plont","name":"Face_Plont","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1883_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-20T19:40:34-07:00","id":634173,"title":"","content":"I am trying to figure out how to use the antagonists in the 2e book to be deadly to werewolves before pulling out the big monsters. It would be good to have a benchmark of how many hosts or claimed one can throw at a pack to create a fair fight. <br /> <br /> I guess one can go back to the old default of using silver bullets to make a really lethal encounter, though with the new rules that could lead to mayhem as everyone flies into the death rage.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1107-maryshelly","name":"maryshelly","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1107_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"337"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-20T19:49:30-07:00","id":634177,"title":"","content":"It's really going to depend on the pack, considering some might be full of rahu with full combat builds and experimental mechanics from the Hurt Locker while others might be more diplomatic or mental characters whose sole combat effectiveness comes from shapechanging.<br /> <br /> Even if you accounted for that it's going to depend on the intelligence of the encounter. Did the wolves stumble onto one ratman in a dead end alley? Or did they lure the werewolves to a run-down tenement where beshilu can conceivably come pouring out the walls by the hundreds? Werewolves are predators, they hunt, but azlu are trappers. Odds are if a werewolf sees one it's because the azlu wanted to be seen so that it could lure them into its web.<br /> <br /> Looking at the spirits, the Sparkblood Seneschal shouldn't be hard at all to make into an interesting combat encounter, even if it's not necessarily meant to be part of one. The Shadow Wraiths also could work from both angles, following the storytelling hint of having them in groups of three you have them instigate those around the pack with fear, or instill paranoia in the pack about those around them. The cop who's passed by their lair every day for months now pauses, looking in the windows suspiciously. Some ruffians at the corner look increasingly capable of violence and that glint of metal may be silver.<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":886,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","user":"nofather","date":". 08-20-2015, 11:03 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","name":"nofather","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar886_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11016"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-24T18:54:42-07:00","id":638628,"title":"","content":"I did some scenarios with a single werewolf fighting a rank 3, 4, and then 5 Spirit. I made him kind of tough, 3 in Brawl, 3 in Strength, and a Stamina plus Primal Urge of 5. He then fought it out with the 3 spirits, one at a time, here were the results.<br /> <br /> Rank 3 spirit : The Spirit won, but the werewolf was getting bad rolls. He was able to hit him once and inflict damage. I figure if he was tougher and luckier, he might have won.<br /> <br /> Rank 4 spirit: The Spirit won, the werewolf was throwing chance dice and even botched once. The Spirit was able to crush him after he finished his Garou war form.<br /> <br /> Rank 5 spirit: The Spirit won, This was the scenario where the spirit might have been able to cause aggravated damage to the werewolf when he was in garou form. He was able to squish him like a bug once the werewolf was forced to change forms. <br /> <br /> Generally, the werewolf was able to shift into Garou form at the beginning of the battle and enjoy 5 rounds of power. After that, he shifted into Urshul form to continue the fight. Against a rank 3 spirit, he might have been able to cause more damage, but he was missing many of his hits. The rank 4 and 5 spirits were still chance die and were unable to do anything. Here were some impressions<br /> <br /> -I can see why the garou form was changed to have super powered regeneration, without it, the werewolf would have been completely demolished earlier on.<br /> <br /> - Assuming the garou form made me think of Pac Man eating a power pellet, he is invincible for about 10 seconds and all the ghosts are afraid of him, but when that time runs out he is very vulnerable. I also had images in my mind of Mario turning into Super Mario for a short time, not sure if that is thematically correct...<br /> <br /> - I only had one werewolf so he was at a disadvantage. I am sure a pack would have helped, but sometimes I wonder if the whole pack would need to turn to garou at the same time to bring down some of the bigger spirits...what if they can not in the time allocated?<br /> <br /> - I can now see Kuruth as being the big red button, the ultimate temptation. I can see several situations where a werewolf is about to run out of rage and still needs to defeat a big enemy. He is tempted to fly into the death rage because that is the only way he can defeat his enemy. Of course, that is when the beast takes over and tragedy happens. I wonder if this was intentional (&quot;I can win this battle if I give into the rage...but at what cost????&quot;)","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1107-maryshelly","name":"maryshelly","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1107_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"337"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-24T20:02:06-07:00","id":638718,"title":"","content":"Your exercise said nothing, first because you didn't seem to have used Gifts, second because you didn't had a Pack, and third because there are too much more factors. There are things you can test in white-rooms, there are things you can't. What you did managed was to show that Rank 3+ Spirits work as they where intended to work.<br /> <br /> But lets see... I'll give you the hard numbers you want.<br /> <br /> At Rank 1, a spirit is just a minion. It should only be a challenge for Average Joe alone in the darkness. A Rank 2 spirit is roughly equal a starting Major Splat, and a combat focused Uratha should be able to overtake most of those, though a starting one can have a hard time against the tougher spirits of the Rank. A Rank 3 spirit is a major problem, meant for an entire pack of starting Uratha or a high level one to take care. A Rank 4 spirit is really dangerous and no Uratha should think to take it alone, unless in possession of a Bane or Ban. A Rank 5 spirit is a challenge for the most experienced packs, and even them with the Ban or Bane at hand just in case.<br /> <br /> Of course, none of that will actually help you. You see, the game works with relatively high lethality, and a lot of circumstantial bonuses. That means that you are plainly ignoring a lot of actually relevant variables. I'm not the one to say that this game doesn't work that way because I think the system is the Holy Grail of role-play. The system doesn't work like that because it makes the variables of combat too unyielding for predictions, first by making a rather large range of values for intrinsic variables (the things in your sheet, whatever they are), and second by making equally large ranges for circumstantial variables. So combat is too context-sensitive for any White-Room prediction to actually work.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-25T19:19:22-07:00","id":639707,"title":"","content":"I was thinking about how to create a mathematical model of a boss fight between a pack and a spirit which might be exciting, but can allow the PC's to win without them defeating their enemy. I tried to do this mathematically. I see that a spirit with a dice pool of about 20 can inflict about 5 health levels of damage to a werewolf after defense (average of 5). Since a werewolf usually has about 10 to 12 health levels in Urshul form, on average, that would mean about 2 hits. However, since a werewolf can &quot;reset&quot; its health by shifting into Garou form, then it would take about 20 health levels of damage to bring one down. <br /> <br /> Against a pack of 5 werewolves, it would mean that over the course of the battle the spirit would need to inflict about 100 levels of damage, or inflict damage for 20 rounds.<br /> <br /> The pack would need to inflict about 20 health levels over the same period of time, assuming the spirit has 20 health. The way to average this out is to make the defense of the spirit high enough so that the <u><i>last werewolf hitting him can roll one dice.</i></u> Let me explain, if the pack is ganging up on a spirit, each one after the first will be able to reduce the spirit defense by 1, what arrangement will allow the last werewolf to hit him to roll 1 dice while the other pack members role chance dice? The reason for rolling one dice is that it will average one hit ever 3 rounds, with 2L due to claws, to cause an average of 1 health level a round. <br /> <br /> I assume that all werewolves will be in Urshul form since that is the popular harrying form. The spirit will have a defense and health high enough to hang on in the fight until it has inflicted enough damage to pummel the pack.<br /> <br /> Of course, in this scenario, the werewolves will win because I did not take into account things like gifts, the Garou form, or any other special abilities they may have. However, it would probably extend the battle a bit and make it exciting since the pack will need to think tactically. The good thing about spirits is that they can buff their stats with essence so they can &quot;power up&quot; to get to that sweet spot.<br /> <br /> If this seems too easy, maybe the spirit can increase it's defense by 1 more to ensure that the <u><i>last werewolf rolls a chance die</i></u>. That would mean that the pack would need to use gifts, the garou form, other abilities, and maybe Kuruth to win the battle. <br /> <br /> Anyways, this was just a brainstorm and my attempt to create a mathematical model to equalize a battle between a pack and a powerful spirit.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1107-maryshelly","name":"maryshelly","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1107_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"337"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-25T19:20:51-07:00","id":639709,"title":"","content":"...the first paragraph should read &quot; can allow the PC's to win without defeating their enemy too easily&quot;","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1107-maryshelly","name":"maryshelly","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1107_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"337"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-25T20:24:01-07:00","id":639777,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>maryshelly</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/568011-balanced-combat-encounters-for-a-pack-in-2nd-ed?p=639707#post639707\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I was thinking about how to create a mathematical model of a boss fight between a pack and a spirit which might be exciting, but can allow the PC's to win without them defeating their enemy. I tried to do this mathematically. I see that a spirit with a dice pool of about 20 can inflict about 5 health levels of damage to a werewolf after defense (average of 5). Since a werewolf usually has about 10 to 12 health levels in Urshul form, on average, that would mean about 2 hits. However, since a werewolf can &quot;reset&quot; its health by shifting into Garou form, then it would take about 20 health levels of damage to bring one down.<br /> <br /> Against a pack of 5 werewolves, it would mean that over the course of the battle the spirit would need to inflict about 100 levels of damage, or inflict damage for 20 rounds.<br /> <br /> The pack would need to inflict about 20 health levels over the same period of time, assuming the spirit has 20 health. The way to average this out is to make the defense of the spirit high enough so that the <u><i>last werewolf hitting him can roll one dice.</i></u> Let me explain, if the pack is ganging up on a spirit, each one after the first will be able to reduce the spirit defense by 1, what arrangement will allow the last werewolf to hit him to roll 1 dice while the other pack members role chance dice? The reason for rolling one dice is that it will average one hit ever 3 rounds, with 2L due to claws, to cause an average of 1 health level a round.<br /> <br /> I assume that all werewolves will be in Urshul form since that is the popular harrying form. The spirit will have a defense and health high enough to hang on in the fight until it has inflicted enough damage to pummel the pack.<br /> <br /> Of course, in this scenario, the werewolves will win because I did not take into account things like gifts, the Garou form, or any other special abilities they may have. However, it would probably extend the battle a bit and make it exciting since the pack will need to think tactically. The good thing about spirits is that they can buff their stats with essence so they can &quot;power up&quot; to get to that sweet spot.<br /> <br /> If this seems too easy, maybe the spirit can increase it's defense by 1 more to ensure that the <u><i>last werewolf rolls a chance die</i></u>. That would mean that the pack would need to use gifts, the garou form, other abilities, and maybe Kuruth to win the battle.<br /> <br /> Anyways, this was just a brainstorm and my attempt to create a mathematical model to equalize a battle between a pack and a powerful spirit.</div> </div> </div> </div>Dunno about your players but I'd call bullshit. The typical Rahu got like 18 dice to chew face off the bat. This means the spirit would have to have like over 20 defense? That's 20+ Power &amp; Finesse in 2e.<br /> <br /> You forget stuff like Loci, broods, influence and numina. What happens when the antagonist, instead of just fighting head-on, just steal a character physical Touchstone's body as a nanutari?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/576-ephsy","name":"Ephsy","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar576_2.png","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2615"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-25T20:54:13-07:00","id":639821,"title":"","content":"Providing they're higher thank rank 1, spirit Defense is the lesser of Power or Finesse unless they have the Stalwart Numina, which lets them use their Resistance. At ranks 2 and higher they have 9-14, 15-25, 26-35, 36-45 Attribute dots to divide among their three Attributes. As far as I've seen in the books they don't usually have an equal divide, but they don't usually have that much of a variance, either, unless one aspect is essential to their Influence. There's a Maximum Attribute limit of 7, 9, 12, and 15 at those ranks. So at the highest end of rank 5, you're going to have, at most but not counting temporary 'buffs,' a spirit with a 15/15/15 spread with a Defense of 15.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","name":"nofather","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar886_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11016"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-26T23:28:55-07:00","id":641208,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>maryshelly</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/568011-balanced-combat-encounters-for-a-pack-in-2nd-ed?p=639707#post639707\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I was thinking about how to create a mathematical model of a boss fight between a pack and a spirit which might be exciting, but can allow the PC's to win without them defeating their enemy. I tried to do this mathematically. I see that a spirit with a dice pool of about 20 can inflict about 5 health levels of damage to a werewolf after defense (average of 5). Since a werewolf usually has about 10 to 12 health levels in Urshul form, on average, that would mean about 2 hits. However, since a werewolf can &quot;reset&quot; its health by shifting into Garou form, then it would take about 20 health levels of damage to bring one down.<br /> <br /> Against a pack of 5 werewolves, it would mean that over the course of the battle the spirit would need to inflict about 100 levels of damage, or inflict damage for 20 rounds.<br /> <br /> The pack would need to inflict about 20 health levels over the same period of time, assuming the spirit has 20 health. The way to average this out is to make the defense of the spirit high enough so that the <u><i>last werewolf hitting him can roll one dice.</i></u> Let me explain, if the pack is ganging up on a spirit, each one after the first will be able to reduce the spirit defense by 1, what arrangement will allow the last werewolf to hit him to roll 1 dice while the other pack members role chance dice? The reason for rolling one dice is that it will average one hit ever 3 rounds, with 2L due to claws, to cause an average of 1 health level a round.<br /> <br /> I assume that all werewolves will be in Urshul form since that is the popular harrying form. The spirit will have a defense and health high enough to hang on in the fight until it has inflicted enough damage to pummel the pack.<br /> <br /> Of course, in this scenario, the werewolves will win because I did not take into account things like gifts, the Garou form, or any other special abilities they may have. However, it would probably extend the battle a bit and make it exciting since the pack will need to think tactically. The good thing about spirits is that they can buff their stats with essence so they can &quot;power up&quot; to get to that sweet spot.<br /> <br /> If this seems too easy, maybe the spirit can increase it's defense by 1 more to ensure that the <u><i>last werewolf rolls a chance die</i></u>. That would mean that the pack would need to use gifts, the garou form, other abilities, and maybe Kuruth to win the battle.<br /> <br /> Anyways, this was just a brainstorm and my attempt to create a mathematical model to equalize a battle between a pack and a powerful spirit.</div> </div> </div> </div>Not withstand the errors other found, the problem with such mathematical model, in actually any game, is that it is based on mean, and then you have to take into account average absolute deviation to understand how much actual play can diverge from predicted behavior.<br /> <br /> At every game system, you'll have statistical dispersion of results. In an actual game, the outcome can deviate from the expectation in both frequency and intensity. In WoD's case, the variables involved vary too much, making for too great an average absolute deviation. So most games will deviate considerably from mean, and <u>that</u> completely kills predictability on most practical levels.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-31T16:14:02-07:00","id":647348,"title":"","content":"I think designing interesting encounters is more about looking at each of your PCs and making sure that all of them have something interesting to do. I mean, the PCs will never read your mind and do what you expdct, but you still need to work off the pre-conceived notion that each PC needs to be useful in some way.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1157-soulgambit","name":"SoulGambit","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"912"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}